{
  "answer": "The exit interface is the physical or logical interface through which a packet leaves the router, while the next-hop is the IP address of the next device in the path to the destination. Specifying a static route with only an exit interface (e.g., 'ip route 0.0.0.0 0.0.0.0 Ethernet0') causes the router to treat all destinations as directly connected, performing ARP for each destination, which can lead to high CPU and ARP cache issues. In contrast, specifying a next-hop IP address (optionally with an interface) prevents this and is generally recommended.",
  "citations": ["lotte_technology_search_100k_c627537_0", "lotte_technology_search_100k_c621714_1", "lotte_technology_search_100k_c621714_2", "lotte_technology_search_100k_c621714_3", "lotte_technology_search_100k_c621714_4", "lotte_technology_search_100k_c621714_5", "lotte_technology_search_100k_c621714_6"],
  "insufficient_context": false
}

{
  "answer": "Based on the retrieved context, an exit interface is the local interface through which a packet is sent, while a next-hop is the destination router or address the packet is forwarded to. The context explains that forwarding decides which exit interface to use to send a packet to the next hop, but does not provide a detailed comparison. Therefore, the retrieved context is insufficient for a complete differentiation.",
  "citations": ["lotte_technology_search_100k_c627537"],
  "insufficient_context": true
}

{
  "answer": "The exit interface is the outgoing interface on a router used to forward packets, while the next-hop is the IP address of the next router in the path. Pointing a static route to an exit interface causes the router to treat all destinations as directly connected and perform ARP for each, whereas specifying a next-hop IP address avoids this by using the interface associated with that IP.",
  "citations": ["lotte_technology_search_100k_c627537", "lotte_technology_search_100k_c621714"],
  "insufficient_context": false
}

