{
  "answer": "The difference is that older iPhone models (like iPhone 4) have a mechanical home button that physically moves when pressed, while newer models (iPhone 7/7 Plus/8/8 Plus) have a static button that uses a Taptic Engine to simulate a click via haptic feedback, rather than actual movement.",
  "citations": ["1", "2", "3", "6", "7", "8", "9", "18", "21", "29", "44"],
  "insufficient_context": false
}

{
  "answer": "Older iPhones (e.g., iPhone 4/5/6) have a mechanical home button that physically clicks when pressed. Starting with iPhone 7, 7 Plus, 8, and 8 Plus, the home button is a solid-state force-sensitive sensor that uses a Taptic Engine to provide haptic feedback simulating a click; it does not physically move. Later models (iPhone X and later) have no home button, relying on gestures.",
  "citations": ["8", "9", "19", "6", "18", "12"],
  "insufficient_context": false
}

{
  "answer": "Older iPhones (e.g., iPhone 4, 5, 6) have a mechanical home button that physically clicks when pressed. Starting with iPhone 7 and 8 series, the home button is static (does not move) and uses a force touch sensor with the Taptic Engine to provide a simulated haptic click as feedback. Newer iPhone models without a home button rely on Face ID.",
  "citations": ["lotte_technology_search_100k_c106444"],
  "insufficient_context": false
}

{
  "answer": "The difference is that older iPhone models have a mechanical home button that physically moves and clicks, while iPhone 7, iPhone 7 Plus, iPhone 8, and iPhone 8 Plus have a static home button that uses the Taptic Engine to provide haptic feedback, simulating a click without physical movement.",
  "citations": ["lotte_technology_search_100k_c106444"],
  "insufficient_context": false
