}

{
  "answer": "In product management, a \"function\" is the purpose or reason something exists (e.g., getting from place A to place B), while a \"feature\" is a product behavior that a user can interact with (e.g., steering wheel). Features are visible to users and sold by sales, whereas functions are often transparent to users and implemented by developers. There is no strict hierarchy, and one feature may support multiple functions.",
  "citations": ["6", "1", "5", "15", "21", "22"],
  "insufficient_context": false
}

{
  "answer": "In product management, a function is the purpose or reason a product exists (e.g., getting from point A to B), while a feature is a user-interactable behavior that supports that function (e.g., steering wheel). Functions are transparent to users and not well-suited for user stories, whereas features are what users interact with and salespeople sell. There is no strict hierarchy; one feature can support multiple functions.",
  "citations": ["5", "10", "4", "18", "15"],
  "insufficient_context": false
}

{
  "answer": "In product management, a function refers to the purpose or goal of a product (e.g., getting from place A to B), while a feature is a specific behavior or component the user interacts with (e.g., steering wheel) to achieve that function. In programming, features are what the program does (user-facing capabilities), whereas functions are implementation details (how features are coded).",
  "citations": ["lotte_technology_search_100k_c466085", "lotte_technology_search_100k_c421305", "lotte_technology_search_100k_c421310"],
  "insufficient_context": false
}

{
  "answer": "In product management, a function refers to the purpose or reason a product exists (e.g., getting from place A to B), while a feature is a specific behavior the user can interact with to achieve that function (e.g., steering wheel, gear stick). Functions are often backend processes that enable features, and features are what salespeople sell, whereas functions are what programmers develop.",
  "citations": ["lotte_technology_search_100k_c466085", "lotte_technology_search_100k_c421309", "lotte_technology_search_100k_c421310"],
  "insufficient_context": false
}

{
  "answer": "Off-brand iPhone chargers can be bad due to increased risks of fire, electric shock, and potential damage to your device. Low-cost knockoffs and counterfeit chargers often use cheap parts, lack safety mechanisms, and have high failure rates (e.g., 99% failure rate noted in one study). However, not all third-party chargers are unsafe; quality varies, and some may work fine, but it's hard to determine which are safe. Using a charger that is not Apple-certified carries risks, and official Apple chargers are generally more reliable.",
  "citations": ["1", "2", "3", "5", "6"],
  "insufficient_context": false
}

{
  "answer": "Off-brand iPhone chargers carry higher risks of fire and electric shock due to cheap components and poor construction, but not all are unsafe. Some are generally safe, but there is no reliable way to identify which ones. It's safer to use certified or high-quality chargers.",
  "citations": ["lotte_technology_search_100k_c97930", "lotte_technology_search_100k_c28591", "lotte_technology_search_100k_c95192", "lotte_technology_search_100k_c28899", "lotte_technology_search_100k_c96899", "lotte_technology_search_100k_c6155"],
  "insufficient_context": false
}

