{
  "answer": "To delete photos from iCloud but keep them on your computer, first copy the photos to a separate folder on your computer (e.g., desktop). Then, turn off iCloud Photo Library in your device settings (e.g., System Preferences > iCloud > Photos on Mac, or Settings > iCloud > Photos on iPhone). This stops syncing, so photos on your computer remain while you can delete them from iCloud. Note: If you delete from iCloud while iCloud Photo Library is enabled, they will be removed from all devices.",
  "citations": ["2", "8", "10", "11", "19", "26"],
  "insufficient_context": false
}

{
  "answer": "First, ensure your photos are saved locally on your computer by either exporting them from the Photos app or copying them to a separate folder. Then, delete the photos from your iPhone or other devices, and they will be removed from iCloud as well, but the copies on your computer will remain. You can also turn off iCloud Photo Library in System Preferences > iCloud to stop syncing.",
  "citations": ["1", "6", "9", "14"],
  "insufficient_context": false
}

{
  "answer": "To delete photos from iCloud but keep them on your computer, first transfer the photos to a local folder on your computer (e.g., by dragging them from the Photos app or iCloud folder to a desktop folder). After confirming the copies are saved, you can delete the photos from iCloud (e.g., by deleting them from the Photos app or iCloud library). Alternatively, disable iCloud Photo Library to stop syncing, which keeps local photos intact while photos are removed from iCloud. Note that with iCloud Photo Library enabled, deleting photos from one device typically removes them from all synced devices, so local backup before deletion is essential.",
