{
  "answer": "Emulated files on Android are a virtual representation of files stored on the actual filesystem (ext4 or f2fs) at `/data/media`. They are exposed through an emulated filesystem (e.g., `/storage/emulated/0/`) that serves two main purposes: retaining USB connectivity to PCs via MTP and enforcing permission restrictions to protect user privacy. The emulation allows the same file to be accessed with different permission sets for different apps and users, and is implemented using FUSE or sdcardfs.",
  "citations": ["1", "10", "40", "13", "22", "53", "55"],
  "insufficient_context": false
}

{
  "answer": "Emulated files on Android are files that appear under paths like /storage/emulated/0/ but are actually stored on the real filesystem (e.g., /data/media/0/) and exposed through an emulated virtual filesystem. This abstraction layer enforces permissions and maintains compatibility with FAT-based storage while using ext4/f2fs underneath.",
  "citations": ["lotte_technology_search_100k_c165525", "lotte_technology_search_100k_c165505"],
  "insufficient_context": false
}

{
  "answer": "Emulated files on Android are stored in the virtual filesystem at /storage/emulated/0/, which is actually an abstraction layer over the real filesystem at /data/media/0/. This emulation provides FAT-like behavior (e.g., symlinks and compatible filenames) while enforcing Unix permissions and access restrictions for apps and USB/MTP connectivity.",
  "citations": ["lotte_technology_search_100k_c165525"],
  "insufficient_context": false
}

