{
  "answer": "The URG flag is a 1-bit field in the TCP header that indicates the segment contains urgent data. When set, the receiving station evaluates the urgent pointer, a 16-bit field, which indicates the number of bytes from the start of the segment that are urgent. The urgent flag signals prioritization, while the pointer specifies the exact location of the urgent data. In contrast to the PSH flag, which follows FIFO ordering, the URG flag allows urgent data to bypass previously buffered data.",
