{
  "answer": "iBGP is used to share routes learned via eBGP among all routers within an autonomous system (AS). It provides scalability and control for handling large routing tables (e.g., full internet routing) that IGPs cannot handle, and offers advanced policy features like BGP communities and local-pref. iBGP is necessary because eBGP routes are not automatically propagated internally; without it, you would have to redistribute all eBGP routes into an IGP, which is impractical.",
  "citations": ["1", "5", "9", "13", "19", "22"],
  "insufficient_context": false
}

{
  "answer": "iBGP is used within an autonomous system to distribute routes learned via eBGP to all internal routers, enabling consistent routing policies (e.g., local preference, communities) and scalability by avoiding redistributing the full Internet routing table into an IGP like OSPF, which cannot handle that many routes. Without iBGP, each eBGP-speaking router would only know its own external routes, preventing internal routers from making optimal exit path selections.",
  "citations": ["1", "3", "5", "7"],
  "insufficient_context": false
}

{
  "answer": "iBGP is used over eBGP for internal route distribution within an AS to achieve scalability (handling hundreds of thousands of routes without overloading IGPs like OSPF), enforce control and flexibility (using BGP attributes such as communities, local preference, and filtering), and propagate eBGP-learned routes to all routers without redistributing into the IGP.",
  "citations": ["lotte_technology_search_100k_c621034", "lotte_technology_search_100k_c621010"],
  "insufficient_context": false
