{
  "answer": "To check if a port is blocked on Ubuntu, you can use commands like `netstat -tuplen | grep <port>`, `ss -lntu | grep <port>`, `nmap -sT localhost -p <port>`, `lsof -i:<port>`, and `iptables -nL | grep <port>`. These tools help determine if the port is open, closed, or filtered. For example, `netstat` shows listening services, `nmap` can indicate 'filtered' if a firewall blocks probes, and `iptables` lists firewall rules.",
  "citations": ["2", "11", "6", "9", "14", "15", "34"],
  "insufficient_context": false
}

{
  "answer": "To check if a port is blocked on Ubuntu, you can:\n1. Check if a service is listening: `sudo netstat -tuplen | grep <port>` or `sudo ss -lntu | grep <port>` or `sudo lsof -i:<port>`.\n2. Check firewall rules: `sudo iptables -L -n | grep <port>`.\n3. Test connectivity from another machine: `telnet <target-ip> <port>` or `nc -vz <target-ip> <port>`. If connection refused, the port may be blocked by a firewall or ISP.\n4. Use nmap to scan: `nmap -p <port> <target-ip>`. If the port shows as 'filtered', it is likely blocked by a firewall.",
  "citations": ["1", "2", "6", "8"],
  "insufficient_context": false
}

{
  "answer": "To check if a port is blocked on Ubuntu, use the following commands:\n- Check if a service is listening: `sudo netstat -tuplen | grep <port>` or `sudo ss -lntu | grep :<port>`.\n- Check firewall rules: `sudo iptables -nL | grep <port>`.\n- Scan ports locally: `nmap -sT localhost` (TCP) or `nmap -sU localhost` (UDP), specifying port with `-p <port>`.\n- Check which process is using the port: `sudo lsof -i :<port>` or `sudo lsof -Pn -i4 | grep :<port>`.\n- Test connectivity from another host: `telnet <target> <port>` or `nc -v <target> <port>` (connection refused may indicate blocking).",
  "citations": [
