{
  "answer": "Forwarding is the general process of moving data (e.g., packets, frames) from one device to the next towards the destination, operating at any layer of the OSI model. Routing is a specific type of forwarding performed at layer 3, where a router uses the destination IP address and its routing table to decide the next hop. In short, routing is layer-3 forwarding.",
  "citations": ["1", "3", "4", "5", "6", "11", "12"],
  "insufficient_context": false
}

{
  "answer": "Forwarding is the general action of moving a datagram from one interface to another to send it toward its destination, applicable at any layer. Routing is a specific type of forwarding decision made at layer 3 using the IP destination address to determine the next hop.",
  "citations": ["4", "1", "5", "11"],
  "insufficient_context": false
}

{
  "answer": "Forwarding is the generic process of moving a protocol data unit (e.g., packet, frame) from one interface to another toward its destination, while routing specifically refers to the layer-3 decision-making process of determining the next hop based on the destination IP address using a routing table. Forwarding can occur at any layer, whereas routing is exclusively a layer-3 function.",
  "citations": ["1", "3", "5"],
  "insufficient_context": false
}

{
  "answer": "Forwarding is the general process of moving a protocol data unit (packet/frame/cell) from one interface to another based on a forwarding table, applicable at any network layer. Routing is a specific type of forwarding that occurs at Layer 3 (network layer), where the forwarding decision is based on the IP destination address. In essence, routing is the layer-3 subset of forwarding.",
  "citations": ["1", "3", "5"],
  "insufficient_context": false
}

