{
  "answer": "Object-oriented programming (OOP) can be hard for several reasons. First, the basic explanation of OOP often has little to do with how it's used in practice [2]. Many programming tools do not facilitate OOP well [3]. It requires abstract thinking, which many people find challenging [9]. Teaching methods are often poor [10], and the concepts like inheritance, interfaces, polymorphism, and composition are numerous and complex [5]. For those coming from procedural programming, unlearning old habits and understanding the 'why' behind OOP can be difficult [17][47]. Additionally, OOP involves upfront design and planning, which many programmers struggle with [14]. The gap between theory and practical application, along with the abstract nature of representing non-physical concepts, adds to the difficulty [42][52].",
  "citations": ["2", "3", "5", "9", "10", "14", "17", "42", "47", "52"],
  "insufficient_context": false
}

{
  "answer": "Object oriented programming is not inherently hard, but several factors contribute to its perceived difficulty. It requires a paradigm shift from procedural thinking ([53]), and inadequate teaching methods can hinder understanding ([7]). A solid grasp of data structures and late binding is necessary ([25]), and the concept often only 'clicks' after practical experience ([46], [48]). Additionally, many learners struggle with the 'why' and how to apply OOP well ([51], [54]), and exposure to poor OOP examples can compound the difficulty ([14]).",
  "citations": ["7", "14", "25", "46", "48", "51", "53", "54"],
  "insufficient_context": false
}

{
  "answer": "Object-oriented programming (OOP) can be hard because it requires abstract thinking (chunks 1, 7), a shift from procedural programming (chunks 3, 4, 6, 8), and poor teaching methods that rely on ineffective metaphors instead of practical design patterns (chunks 6, 8, 9, 10). Additionally, doing OOP well involves difficult decisions about code structure and extensibility (chunk 2), and many learners don't see the benefits until they have enough experience (chunks 4, 6, 10).",
  "citations": ["1", "2", "3", "4", "6", "7", "8", "9", "10"],
  "insufficient_context": false
}

{
  "answer": "Object-oriented programming is often considered hard because it requires a paradigm shift from procedural thinking, poor teaching methods obscure the core concepts, and the real difficulty lies in applying OOP well (e.g., designing reusable and maintainable code). Many learners struggle with understanding the \"why\" behind OOP rather than the mechanics.",
  "citations": ["1", "2", "4", "6", "8"],
  "insufficient_context": false
}

