{
  "answer": "A web API works by allowing a client (like a browser) to make HTTP requests (e.g., GET, POST) to a web server. The server processes the request and returns data, often in JSON or XML format. The URL specifies the resource, and HTTP methods indicate the action (GET to retrieve, POST to create, etc.). This enables interaction between applications over the web.",
  "citations": ["1", "4", "13", "39", "49", "65", "74"],
  "insufficient_context": false
}

{
  "answer": "A web API works when a client (e.g., a web browser) makes an HTTP request to a web server. The server examines the request to determine what the user wants and returns data in some format (like a page). The client then processes the returned data. Different styles exist: in RPC, a single URL is used and requests are made by POSTing a document with function information; in REST, URLs represent objects and HTTP methods (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE) specify actions on those objects.",
  "citations": ["1", "11", "21", "22", "24", "37", "45"],
  "insufficient_context": false
}

{
  "answer": "In a website, a web API works by clients (e.g., a browser) making HTTP requests to a server. The server determines what the client wants and returns data in a structured format like JSON or XML. Common styles include REST, where each URL represents an object and HTTP methods (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE) specify the action, and RPC, where a single URL accepts a document describing the function call. APIs define rules for communication, enabling software programs to interact.",
  "citations": ["1", "3", "4"],
  "insufficient_context": false
}

{
  "answer": "In a website, a web API works when a client (e.g., a web browser) makes an HTTP request to a web server. The server examines the request to determine what the user wants and returns data in a format (like a page) that the client then uses. The client and server must agree on rules, typically using styles like Remote Procedure Call (RPC) or REpresentational State Transfer (REST). In RPC, a single URL is used and requests include a function name and arguments. In REST, each URL within a namespace represents an object, and HTTP methods (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE) are used to perform actions on those objects.",
  "citations": ["lotte_technology_search_100k_c465478"],
  "insufficient_context": false
}

