{
  "answer": "The DHCP offer is broadcast primarily because the client may not yet have an IP address and may be unable to receive unicast IP datagrams until its protocol software is configured. By broadcasting the offer, the client can receive it. Additionally, broadcasting the DHCPREQUEST (which follows the offer) notifies other DHCP servers that might have sent offers that their offer is not taken.",
  "citations": ["1", "4", "7", "9", "17", "22", "27"],
  "insufficient_context": false
}

{
  "answer": "The DHCP offer is not always broadcast; it can be unicast. According to RFC 2131, DHCP servers normally deliver DHCPOFFER messages via unicast to the client's MAC address. However, a client that cannot receive unicast IP datagrams before being configured with an IP address sets the BROADCAST bit in its DHCPDISCOVER message, which causes the server to broadcast the offer. Thus, the offer is broadcast only when the client indicates it cannot handle unicast.",
  "citations": ["lotte_technology_search_100k_c631803", "lotte_technology_search_100k_c629643", "lotte_technology_search_100k_c636953", "lotte_technology_search_100k_c623291", "lotte_technology_search_100k_c623294"],
  "insufficient_context": false
}

{
  "answer": "DHCP offer is broadcast to accommodate clients that cannot receive unicast IP datagrams before their IP stack is configured. According to RFC 2131, the client sets a broadcast flag in its DISCOVER or REQUEST messages if it needs a broadcast reply; otherwise, the server may unicast the offer. Thus, broadcasting ensures delivery when the client lacks IP configuration.",
  "citations": ["4", "5", "8"],
  "insufficient_context": false
}

