{
  "answer": "MTU must match in OSPF because mismatched MTU prevents OSPF adjacency formation—the Database Description packet is rejected if the interface MTU indicates a larger size than the receiving router can accept without fragmentation ([28]). Additionally, frames larger than the receive MTU are silently discarded, causing packet loss ([10]). OSPF requires valid Layer2 adjacencies and matching MTUs to ensure a reliable forwarding path ([1], [16], [19]).",
  "citations": ["1", "28", "10", "16", "19"],
  "insufficient_context": false
}

{
  "answer": "MTU should match in OSPF primarily because OSPF requires it for adjacency formation and to ensure Database Description packets are accepted without fragmentation. Mismatched MTU can cause packets to be silently discarded, leading to adjacency failures and routing instability. Additionally, matching MTU avoids the performance penalties of IP fragmentation, which increases CPU burden and can degrade convergence. The OSPF RFC mandates this check to enforce stable and fast routing protocol operation.",
  "citations": ["1", "27", "16", "73", "89", "9"],
  "insufficient_context": false
}

{
  "answer": "MTU must match in OSPF because OSPF does not support fragmentation and reassembly for its protocol packets. If the Interface MTU field in a Database Description packet indicates an IP datagram size larger than the router can accept without fragmentation, the packet is rejected, preventing adjacency formation. Additionally, mismatched MTUs can lead to black-holing traffic, as frames larger than the receive MTU are silently discarded, and there is no Layer2 MTU discovery mechanism to resolve such mismatches. Ensuring matching MTUs helps maintain protocol stability and fast convergence.",
  "citations": ["1", "2"],
  "insufficient_context": false
}

{
  "answer": "MTU must match in OSPF because OSPF relies on Database Description packets to establish adjacencies; if the MTU in these packets exceeds the receiving interface's capability, the packet is rejected, preventing adjacency formation. Additionally, mismatched MTUs can cause larger frames to be silently discarded, leading to traffic black-holing and instability in the routing protocol.",
  "citations": ["lotte_technology_search_100k_c623395", "lotte_technology_search_100k_c623396"],
  "insufficient_context": false
}

